\subsection{Related Works}\label{sec:related}
\paragraph{MaxWeight and asymptotic optimality.}  
The MaxWeight (or backpressure) policy, first proposed by \citet{tassiulas1992stability}, is celebrated for its throughput-optimality in general SPNs.  Subsequent work has refined its delay guarantees in various settings via fluid and diffusion approximations~\citep{stolyar2004maxweight,mandelbaum2004scheduling,dai2005maximum,dai2008asymptotic,kang2013diffusion}.  These classical analyses offer elegant asymptotic insights and a strong foundation, yet finite-time guarantees remain critical for modern, time-sensitive applications.

\paragraph{Finite‐horizon analyses in queueing.}
Analyzing queueing behavior over finite horizons remains challenging, even for simple models like the $M/M/1$ queue~\citep{abate1987transient}. Most existing work addresses uncontrolled systems, using tools such as coupling~\citep{robert2013stochastic} and spectral methods~\citep{gamarnik2013rate, van2011transient} to study convergence to steady state. In contrast, finite-horizon analysis for controlled systems is far more limited, with only a few results for routing policies like Join-the-Shortest-Queue (JSQ)~\citep{luczak2006maximum, ma2025convergence}. The finite-time behavior of general scheduling policies remains largely unexamined.


\paragraph{Parameter learning in queueing.}
A growing body of work studies queueing systems where key parameters—such as arrival and service rates—are unknown and must be learned online. A common performance metric is queueing regret, which quantifies the excess queue length incurred by a learning algorithm relative to an oracle policy with complete knowledge of the system~\citep{krishnasamy2021learning,krishnasamy2018learning,stahlbuhk2021learning,freund2023quantifying}. Other works use the time-averaged queue length over a finite horizon to evaluate learning efficiency~\citep{yang2023learning}. In contrast, our work assumes full knowledge of the system parameters and aims to characterize the fundamental gap between the finite-horizon delay incurred by a scheduling policy and the ideal baseline of zero.


\paragraph{Lower bounds for structured dynamic programming.}
Queueing control is a structured dynamic programming problem, yet solving such problems is often intractable due to the exponential growth of state and action spaces—the so-called “curse of dimensionality”~\citep{papadimitriou1987complexity, mundhenk2000complexity}. These computational barriers motivate the study of fundamental performance limits. Despite its importance, prior work on delay lower bounds in queueing systems is limited; a notable exception is \citet{gupta2009delay}, who derive lower bounds on delay in multi-hop networks by reducing to the delay of a $G/D/1$ queue, though this remains challenging to analyze in finite time. We establish the first information-theoretic lower bound on finite-horizon queueing delay, setting a benchmark for evaluating scheduling policies.


\paragraph{Drift‐method limitations and alternatives.}
The Lyapunov drift framework underlies most stability and steady-state analyses in queueing systems~\citep{eryilmaz2012asymptotically, maguluri2016heavy}, supporting the asymptotic optimality of many drift-based methods. However, these methods typically rely on coarse first-order approximations, which often obscure transient inefficiencies and fail to directly optimize key performance metrics. Extensions such as drift-plus-penalty~\citep{neely2010stochastic} introduce auxiliary objectives (e.g., delay or energy), but still lack explicit finite-time performance guarantees. In contrast, we propose a scheduling policy that minimizes the full Lyapunov drift—capturing both first- and second-order terms—to optimize the performance metric in finite time.





\section{Problem Setup and Minimax Framework}
\label{sec:setup}


\subsection{Problem Setup of Queueing Control}
We denote by $\mathbb{R}_+^n$ the set of nonnegative vectors in $\mathbb{R}^n$. For any finite set $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathbb{R}^n$, $\text{conv}(\mathcal{D})$ denotes the convex hull of $\mathcal{D}$. We use $\mathbf{0}$ to denote the zero vector, with the dimension understood from the context. Given two vectors $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ $\max\{x, y\}$ denotes their componentwise maximum, that is, $(\max\{x, y\})_i = \max\{x_i, y_i\}$ for each $i$. Let  $\text{Var}(A)$ denote the variance of the random variable $A$.

We consider a discrete-time single-hop SPN with $n$ parallel queues, where jobs arrive exogenously and depart after a single service. Let $Q(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^n$ denote the queue length vector at discrete time $t$, and $A(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^n$ the arrival vector, where $A_i(t)$ is the number of jobs arriving to queue $i$ for $1\leq i\leq n$. Let $\lambda(t) = \mathbb{E}[A(t)]$ denote the mean arrival rate vector at time $t$.

\paragraph{Queueing Dynamics and Scheduling Set:} The system's dynamics are governed by a scheduling set $\mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathbb{R}_+^n$. Each element of $\mathcal{D}$, termed a \emph{schedule}, specifies the number of jobs that can depart from each queue in a single time slot. At each discrete time $t$, the decision maker selects a schedule $D(t) \in \mathcal{D}$, and the queue lengths evolve according to the following recursion:
\begin{align}\label{queue_recursion}
  Q(t+1) = \max\{Q(t) - D(t), \mathbf{0}\} + A(t), \quad \text{for } t \geq 0,  
\end{align}
with the initial condition $Q(0) = \mathbf{0}$. 



\paragraph{Arrival Processes:} We consider the both \textbf{adversarial} and \textbf{stochastic} arrivals. For \textbf{adversarial  arrivals}, the arrivals $\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ may be chosen by an adversary, possibly with arbitrary dependencies across queues and across time. In particular, an important special case (which is assumed in all asymptotic analysis in the queueing literature) is \textbf{stochastic arrivals}: $\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ is an independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) sequence with a known mean arrival rate vector $\lambda$, i.e., $\lambda(t) = \lambda$ for all $t \geq 0$. We establish both upper and lower bounds under general arrival processes, and additionally derive bounds for the special case of independent arrivals commonly assumed in classical asymptotic results.


% We provide both upper and lower bounds for systems with either dependent or independent arrivals across queues, whereas classical asymptotic results typically assume queue-wise independence.

\begin{definition}
Define the {\em capacity region} $\Pi$ associated with the scheduling set $\mathcal{D}$ as:
\[
\Pi = \{ \gamma \in \mathbb{R}_+^n : \gamma \leq d, \text{ for some } d \in \text{conv}(\mathcal{D}) \}.
\]
\end{definition}
The capacity region $\Pi$ consists of all arrival rates that can be served (or dominated) by at least one schedule in $\mathcal{D}$. We adopt the following standard assumption \citep{stolyar2004maxweight,walton2021learning}.
\begin{assumption}
    $\lambda(t) \in \Pi \ \text{for all} \  t\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{0\}$.
\end{assumption}
\paragraph{Policy:} A \emph{policy} $\Phi$ is defined as a sequence of scheduling rules, $\Phi=\{\phi_t\}_{t \geq 0}$, where each $\phi_t$ is a  mapping from the history up to time $t$, denoted by
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{H}_{t}=\{Q(0),D(0), A(0),\dots, Q(t-1),D(t-1), A(t-1), Q(t)\},
\end{align*}
to a probability distribution over the scheduling set $\mathcal{D}$. At each time $t$, given the history $\mathcal{H}_{t}$, a schedule $D(t)$ is chosen randomly according to the distribution $\phi_t(\mathcal{H}_{t})$.

Our goal is to minimize the cumulative queue length. To achieve this, we analyze the fundamental limits of existing scheduling policies (notably the MaxWeight policy), and develop a novel Lyapunov-based policy that explicitly accounts for second-order terms of the Lyapunov drift, demonstrating optimality against information-theoretic lower bounds in the finite-time horizon regime.




\subsection{Minimax Criteria}
The minimax criterion is a standard approach to studying the intrinsic difficulty of problems in statistics and machine learning \citep{wald1945statistical}. In this work, we extend this criterion to the domain of queueing control, aiming to bridge queueing theory with learning-theoretic methodologies and to motivate the use of statistical decision-theoretic tools in a broader class of dynamic programming problems.
\begin{comment}
Originating in the work of Wald \citep{wald1945statistical}, the minimax approach formalized the connection to von Neumann’s game-theoretic framework \citep{copeland1945john} and provided a unified treatment of statistical estimation and hypothesis testing within the paradigm of statistical decision theory. In this work, we extend this perspective to the domain of queueing control, introducing—for the first time to our knowledge—a minimax formulation for analyzing the finite-time fundamental limits of scheduling policies. This aims to bridge queueing theory with learning-theoretic methodologies and to motivate the application of statistical decision-theoretic tools to a broader class of dynamic programming problems.
\end{comment}


\paragraph{Performance Metrics:} 
We consider two standard performance metrics: the total queue length (see~\citet{neely2010stochastic}), which captures the overall system backlog, and the sum of squares of queue lengths (see~\citet{stolyar2004maxweight}), which reflects the degree of imbalance across queues. These are formally defined as
\begin{align*}
 \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right] \quad \text{and} \quad
\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)^2\right] 
\end{align*}
respectively. In this work, we primarily present results for the total queue length; corresponding matching bounds for the sum of squares can be derived directly via the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality. Note that this is a dynamic programming problem, where the objective at time $t$ is a function of all past decisions $D(0), D(1),...,D(t-1)$. A central challenge in dynamic programming and control is to develop solutions that are both tractable and practically effective. 



\paragraph{Model Classes: Arrival Process and Scheduling Set.}
The queueing system under consideration is defined by an arrival process $\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ and a scheduling set $\mathcal{D}$. Adopting a minimax perspective,  we define the following model class that includes adversarial variance-constrained arrival processes and capacity-constrained scheduling sets:
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2) = \bigg\{(A(\cdot),\mathcal{D}): \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{Var}(A_i(t)) \leq C^2, \; \forall t\geq 0; 
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_i^2 \leq B^2, \; \forall d\in \mathcal{D}
\bigg\},
\end{align*}
and a smaller subclass that captures the case of independent arrivals across queues:
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{M_I}(C^2, B^2) = \big\{(A(\cdot),\mathcal{D}) \subset \mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2): \{A_i(t)\}_{i=1}^{n} \text{are independent}, \forall t\geq 0 \big\},
\end{align*}
where $C\geq 0$ controls the variability of the arrivals across queues and $B>0$ constrains the capacity of the scheduling set.

We aim to characterize the fundamental minimax value of the expected total queue length at time $T$:  
\begin{align}\label{minmax_frame}
   \inf_{\Phi} \sup_{(A(\cdot),\mathcal{D}) \in \mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)} \mathbb{E}_{\Phi, (A(\cdot),\mathcal{D})}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right]. 
\end{align}
This work introduces, for the first time to the best of our knowledge, a minimax formulation for characterizing the finite-time fundamental limits of queueing control, offering a principled approach to quantifying the hardness of structured dynamic scheduling problems.


\section{Information‑Theoretic Lower Bounds}
\label{sec:lower}

In this section, we derive finite-time information-theoretic lower bounds on the expected total queue length for the system introduced in Section \ref{sec:setup}. Formally, we seek a lower bound for the quantity \eqref{minmax_frame}. To this end, we leverage classical information-theoretic methods and minimax analyses from statistical learning theory. The resulting bounds explicitly quantify how queueing backlogs scale with  the  time horizon $T$, the statistical properties of the arrival process, and the capacity constraints imposed by the scheduling set.

Intuitively, the lower bound provides insights into   difficult scenarios an adversary can create under prescribed variance constraints on the arrival processes and capacity constraints on the scheduling set, thereby establishing fundamental performance limits for {\em any} scheduling policy in single‑hop SPNs. 



The following theorem establishes a lower bound under any scheduling policy within the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$.
\begin{theorem}[Information-Theoretic Lower Bound--General Case]\label{thm_lower_bound}
For any scheduling policy, and for arrival processes and scheduling sets within the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$, the following bound holds. When $C=0$, for all $T\geq 1$,
\begin{align}
  \inf_{\Phi} \sup_{\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right] \geq \sqrt{n}B.
\end{align} 
When $C>0$, 
\begin{align}\label{ieq:lower_bound_dependent}
 \inf_{\Phi} \sup_{\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right]\geq 
 \left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
     \frac{n^{3/2}C^2(T-1)}{B(1+nC^2/B^2)^{T-1}}+\sqrt{n}B,  & 1\leq T \leq \frac{B^2+2nC^2}{nC^2} \vspace{.5em}\\
    \frac{e^{\xi(T;n,B,C)}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}(1-\delta(T;n,B,C))nC\sqrt{T-2}+\sqrt{n}B,  & T> \frac{B^2+2nC^2}{nC^2}
 \end{array}  \right. 
\end{align}
with $\xi(T;n,B,C)=\frac{-(B^2+nC^2)^2(T-2)}{12(nC^2(T-2)-B^2)(B^2(T-2)-nC^2)}$ and $\delta(T;n,B,C)=\max\left\{\frac{B^2+nC^2}{B^2(T-1)}, \frac{B^2+nC^2}{nC^2(T-1)}\right\}$. In particular,

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all $T>\frac{2B^2}{nC^2}+\frac{2nC^2}{B^2}+5$, we have the following simpler bound, 
\begin{align}\label{ieq:lower_bound_dependent_constant}
 \inf_{\Phi} \sup_{\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right]\geq 
    \frac{nC\sqrt{T-2}}{2\sqrt{2e\pi}}+\sqrt{n}B;   
\end{align}
\item  As $T\rightarrow \infty$, 
\begin{align}\label{ieq:lower_bound_dependent_limit}
\inf_{\Phi} \sup_{\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)} \liminf_{T\rightarrow \infty}\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right]}{\sqrt{T-2}}\geq 
\frac{nC }{\sqrt{2\pi}}.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{remark}
   In the regime $B \gg \sqrt{n}C$, the temporal window appearing in the first clause of \eqref{ieq:lower_bound_dependent} is sufficiently long and must be explicitly taken into account. When $B \geq \sqrt{n}C$, the exponential term satisfies  
   \[
   1 \leq \left(1 + \frac{nC^2}{B^2}\right)^{T-1}
   \leq \left(1 + \frac{nC^2}{B^2}\right)^{1 + B^2/(nC^2)}
   \leq 2e.
   \]
   Consequently, the following bound holds:
   \[
   \frac{n^{3/2}C^2(T-1)}{2eB}
   \leq
   \frac{n^{3/2}C^2(T-1)}{B\left(1 + nC^2/B^2\right)^{T-1}}
   \leq
   \frac{n^{3/2}C^2(T-1)}{B}.
   \]
   This shows that the lower bound in the first clause of \eqref{ieq:lower_bound_dependent} scales as $\Theta(n^{3/2}C^2T/B)$ in this regime.
\end{remark}
\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch.]
 We outline the key steps in establishing the lower bound, with the formal proof provided in the Appendix \ref{appendix_thm_lower_bound_general}.
Fix a scheduling set $\mathcal{D}$ that satisfies the condition in model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$. 
\begin{enumerate}[topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{A policy-independent lower bound: reducing from policy vectors to capacity values.} 
Let $M=\max\left\{\sum_{i=1}^n d_i : d\in \mathcal{D}\right\}$ denote the maximum total number of jobs that can depart from the system in a single time slot. Comparing the cumulative arrivals with the maximum possible cumulative departures up to time $T$, we define
$S(T-1)= \sum_{t=0}^{T-2}X(t)$ with $ X(t)=\sum_{i=1}^nA_i(t)-M$. Then by \eqref{queue_recursion}, for any arrival process $\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ and any scheduling policy, the total queue length satisfies
\begin{align}\label{ieq:lower_policy_independent}
\sum_{i=1}^n Q_i(T)\geq \max\left\{S(T-1), 0\right\}+\sum_{i=1}^nA_i(T-1).
\end{align}
\item \textbf{Constructing hard instances with dependent binary-valued arrivals.} Inspired by lower bounds for expert problems \citep{cesa2006prediction}, we construct $\{A(t)\}$ as to be i.i.d.\ over $t$. 
When the $A_i(t)$ are independent, $\text{Var}(X(t)) \propto n$; if they are fully dependent, $\text{Var}(X(t)) \propto n^2$. To maximize the fluctuation of $S(T-1)$, we choose arrivals that are fully dependent across queues. Consider the following construction of the arrival process $\{A(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$: for each $t$, 
\begin{align*}
    &\mathbb{P}(A_1(t)=K\lambda_1, A_2(t)=K\lambda_2,\dots,A_n(t)=K\lambda_n)=1/K,\\
    &\mathbb{P}(A_1(t)=0, A_2(t)=0,\dots,A_n(t)=0)=(K-1)/K.
\end{align*}
With this construction, $\mathbb{E}[A(t)]=\lambda$ and $\{X(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$ forms an i.i.d.\ sequence of binary random variables. We then select $\lambda\in\mathcal{D}$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i=M$, and choose the tuning parameter $K$ such that the variance constraint in the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$ holds with equality (see Appendix~\ref{appendix_thm_lower_bound_general}  for the details). Consequently, $\mathbb{E}[X(t)]=0$ and $\text{Var}(X(t))=M^2(K-1)$. This construction of such binary random variables  allows us to derive explicit finite-time lower bounds, rather than relying on asymptotic approximations via the central limit theorem.
\item\textbf{Right‐Tail Overshoot Lower Bound for Sums of Binary Random Variables.} We establish a right-tail lower bound for binary sums using nontrivial anti-concentration arguments, leveraging precise control over discrete fluctuations. Specifically, by Lemma \ref{lem:binary_lower_bound} in Appendix \ref{appendix_lemmas}, we obtain a lower bound on $\mathbb{E}\left[\max\left\{S(T-1), 0\right\} \right]$ simply by scaling the corresponding bounds by $M$.

\item\textbf{Constructing hard instance for the scheduling set.}
Construct the scheduling set as
\[
\mathcal{D}=\left\{(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n) \mid  x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_n=\sqrt{n}B, x_i\geq 0\ \text{for} \ 1\leq i\leq n \right\}.
\]
This choice of $\mathcal{D}$ satisfies the constraint of the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$. In this case, we can choose $\lambda=(B/\sqrt{n}, B/\sqrt{n},\dots,B/\sqrt{n})$ so that $M=\sqrt{n}B$, and set $K-1=\frac{nC^2}{B^2}$ (see Appendix~\ref{appendix_thm_lower_bound_general} for the details). Applying the scaling factor $M$ to the lower bounds of Lemma \ref{lem:binary_lower_bound} and then invoking~\eqref{ieq:lower_policy_independent} yields both \eqref{ieq:lower_bound_dependent} and \eqref{ieq:lower_bound_dependent_constant}. The asymptotic bound \eqref{ieq:lower_bound_dependent_limit} follows immediately from \eqref{ieq:lower_bound_dependent}.  
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}




Some classical queueing models assume independent arrivals across queues to facilitate asymptotic analyses~\citep{tassiulas1992stability,harrison1997dynamic,stolyar2004maxweight}. To complement our general analysis, we also present results under this assumption.

\begin{theorem}[Information-Theoretic Lower Bound--Independent Case]
\label{thm:lower_bound_indep}
For any scheduling policy, and for arrival processes and scheduling sets within the model class $\mathcal{M_I}(C^2, B^2)$, we have 
\begin{align}\label{lower_bound_independent}
\inf_{\Phi} \sup_{\mathcal{M_I}(C^2, B^2)} \liminf_{T\rightarrow \infty}\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q_i(T)\right]}{\sqrt{T-1}}\geq 
\frac{\sqrt{n}C }{\sqrt{2\pi}}.
\end{align}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch.]
  We follow the first two steps of Theorem~\ref{thm_lower_bound}, but construct $\{A(t)\}_{t \geq 0}$ with $A_i(t)$ independent across $i$, and apply the central limit theorem (see Appendix~\ref{appendix_thm_lower_bound_indep} for details).
\end{proof}
This lower bound relates to the asymptotic results of \citet{stolyar2004maxweight} in its dependence on $n$ and $C$, though the two are not directly comparable due to differences in the underlying performance metrics.


Theorems \ref{thm_lower_bound} and \ref{thm:lower_bound_indep} reveal that no policy can guarantee a better scaling than $C\sqrt{nT}$ in finite horizon settings, even under favorable assumptions such as independence and bounded variance of arrivals. This establishes a fundamental benchmark against which any scheduling algorithm can be compared. These results formally show that traditional drift-based methods like MaxWeight, which optimize only the first-order Lyapunov drift, inherently fail to match this lower bound in finite-time regimes.

In the next sections, we introduce a novel algorithm that matches this lower bound up to universal constants by explicitly optimizing both the first- and second-order Lyapunov terms, thereby addressing the identified gap in traditional scheduling methods.
\section{Finite‑Time Performance Guarantees}
\label{sec:upper}
\subsection{Optimal Lyapunov Policy}
\label{sec:policy}

We propose a novel scheduling policy that optimizes the full one-step Lyapunov drift at each decision time. Unlike traditional drift-based methods that optimize only first-order terms, our policy anticipates future queue dynamics and directly controls them through a second-order objective, placing greater emphasis on queue balancing and future congestion reduction.

%     Let $Q(t) \in \mathbb{Z}_+^n$ be the current queue length vector at time $t$, and define the empirical average arrival vector up to time $t-1$ as:
% \[
% \hat{A}_i(t) = \frac{1}{t-1} \sum_{j=1}^{t-1} A_i(j), \qquad \text{for } t \ge 2.
% \]

Let $Q(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^n$ be the current queue length vector at time $t$. We define the schedule $D(t)$ as the solution of the following optimization problem:
\[
D(t) \in \argmin_{d \in \mathcal{D}} \sum_{i=1}^n \left( \max\{ Q_i(t) - d_i, 0 \} \right)^2,
\]
with ties broken arbitrarily.
This expression represents a one-step lookahead of the quadratic Lyapunov function $V(x) = \|x\|_2^2$, reflecting the anticipated effect of the schedule $d \in \mathcal{D}$. Specifically, using the queue dynamics in \eqref{queue_recursion}, the objective minimizes the following surrogate of the Lyapunov drift:
\[
\Delta V(t) = \mathbb{E}[ V(Q(t+1)-A(t)) - V(Q(t)-A(t-1)) \mid \mathcal{H}_t ].
\]
 We refer to this policy as the $\LyapOpt$ policy. It is robust to both stochastic and adversarial arrival patterns, and adapts dynamically to empirical traffic statistics. 

Before presenting the main performance guarantee of this policy, we first develop a general Lyapunov drift analysis applicable to any scheduling policy. For any arrival processes and scheduling sets in $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$, and any scheduling sequence $\{D(t)\}_{t\geq 0}$, consider the one-step Lyapunov drift:
\begin{align}
   \Delta V(t) &=\mathbb{E}\left[V\big(Q(t+1)-A(t)\big)-V\big(Q(t)-A(t-1)\big)\mid \mathcal{H}_t\right]\nonumber\\
    &\stackrel{(a)}{=}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^n(\max\{Q_i(t)-D_i(t),0\})^2-\sum_{i=1}^n(Q_i(t)-A_i(t-1))^2 \,\middle |\, \mathcal{H}_t\right]\nonumber\\
    &\leq \sum_{i=1}^n\mathbb{E}\left[(Q_i(t)-D_i(t))^2-Q_i(t)^2+2Q_i(t)A_i(t-1)-A_i(t-1)^2\mid \mathcal{H}_t\right]\nonumber\\
   &=f(Q(t),D(t))+ r(Q(t),A(t-1)), \label{eqn:Delta_Vt}
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
 f(Q(t),d) &:= \mathbb{E}\bigg[\underbrace{2 \sum_{i=1}^n Q_i(t)\big(\lambda_i(t-1) - d_i\big)}_{\text{first-order term}}+\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^n\left(d_i^2 - \lambda_i(t-1)^2\right)}_{\text{second-order term}} \biggm|\mathcal{H}_t\bigg], \label{equ:f_drift+quadratic} 
\end{align}
and $\mathbb{E}[r(Q(t),A(t-1))]=\sum_{i=1}^n\text{Var}(A_i(t-1))$. The first equality (equality $(a)$ in \eqref{eqn:Delta_Vt}) follows from the queue dynamics in \eqref{queue_recursion}, and the remaining expressions are obtained through direct computation (see Appendix \ref{appendix_thm_upper} for details). Taking expectations, summing from  $t = 1$ to $T - 1$ and applying Jensen’s and Cauchy--Schwarz inequalities (see Appendix~\ref{appendix_thm_upper} for the detailed derivation), we obtain:
\begin{align}\label{ieq:upp_bound}
   \mathbb{E}\Big[\sum_{i=1}^n Q_i(T) \Big]\leq 
   n\sqrt{\sum_{t=1}^{T-1} \mathbb{E} [f(Q(t),D(t))]/n+(T-1)C^2}+\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[A_i(T-1)].
\end{align}
We now present the performance guarantee of the $\LyapOpt$ policy.
\begin{theorem}[Finite-Time Performance of the $\LyapOpt$ Policy]
\label{thm:lyapopt}
Within the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$, the $\LyapOpt$ policy achieves the following bound on the expected total queue length: 
 \begin{align}\label{ieq:upp_bound_lyapopt}
   \mathbb{E}\Big[\sum_{i=1}^n Q_i(T) \Big]\leq 
  n \sqrt{\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}\Big[\min_{d\in \mathcal{D}}f(Q(t),d)\Big]/n + (T-1)C^2}+\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[A_i(T-1)]
 \end{align}
with $f(Q(t),d)$ given by \eqref{equ:f_drift+quadratic}. If $\lambda(t) \in \mathcal{D}$ for all $t\geq 0$, the expected total queue length satisfies
\begin{align}\label{upp_bound:achieve_low_bound}
   \mathbb{E}\Big[\sum_{i=1}^n Q_i(T)\Big]\leq 
   n C\sqrt{T-1}+\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[A_i(T-1)].  
 \end{align}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the $\LyapOpt$ policy, the one-step Lyapunov drift
\begin{align*}
    \Delta V(t)&= \min_{d\in \mathcal{D}}\mathbb{E}\Big[\sum_{i=1}^n(\max\{Q_i(t)-d,0\})^2-\sum_{i=1}^n(Q_i(t)-A_i(t-1))^2\,\Big|\, \mathcal{H}_t\Big]\\
    &\leq \min_{d\in \mathcal{D}} f(Q(t),d)+ r(Q(t),A(t-1)).
\end{align*}
Applying a similar derivation as in the proof of \eqref{ieq:upp_bound} yields the bound in \eqref{ieq:upp_bound_lyapopt}.
When $\lambda(t) \in \mathcal{D}$ for all $t \geq 0$, the bound in \eqref{upp_bound:achieve_low_bound} holds since $\min_{d \in \mathcal{D}} f(Q(t), d) \leq 0$.
\end{proof}

From \eqref{ieq:upp_bound_lyapopt}, it is evident that the key advantage  of the $\LyapOpt$ policy lies in the fact that it simultaneously optimizes first-  and second-order terms by minimizing the full Lyapunov drift. This approach allows the departure vector to align more accurately with the arrival rate vector, especially when the scheduling set exhibits geometric irregularities. In contrast, the MaxWeight policy overlooks second-order effects and tends to favor extreme points, which can result in unnecessarily large queue lengths during transient periods.

Specifically, as shown in \eqref{upp_bound:achieve_low_bound}, when the arrival rate lies within the scheduling set $\mathcal{D}$, the $\LyapOpt$ policy perfectly matches the arrival rate--regardless of the geometry of $\mathcal{D}$--and achieves the fundamental lower bound up to a constant factor, thereby establishing its finite-time optimality. 

In Section~\ref{sec:exp}, we empirically validate that this policy dramatically reduces transient queue length compared to MaxWeight policy, and tracks the finite-time lower bound up to constant factors.

\subsection{A Limitation of MaxWeight}
\label{sec:mw-gap}


The MaxWeight scheduling policy is widely recognized for its throughput optimality and asymptotic guarantees. However, in finite-time settings, MaxWeight can exhibit notable performance limitations. In this subsection, we highlight these limitations by presenting established upper-bound results from the literature \citep{tassiulas1992stability,stolyar2004maxweight,neely2010stochastic,walton2021learning}, along with a new lower bound that demonstrates the necessity of the factors appearing in the upper bound and reveals the fundamental limitations of the policy.

\subsubsection{Upper Bound and Physical Meaning of  MaxWeight}

Consider the MaxWeight scheduling policy defined by selecting schedules according to:
\[
D^{\text{MaxWeight}}(t) \in \argmax_{d \in \mathcal{D}} \langle Q(t), d \rangle.
\]
By optimizing the first-order Lyapunov term, MaxWeight prioritizes queues with larger backlogs. The finite-time upper bound on the total queue length under MaxWeight, within the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$, is given below.

\begin{theorem}[Upper Bound of MaxWeight Policy]\label{thm:upper_bound_maxweight}
Under the model class $\mathcal{M}(C^2, B^2)$,  the MaxWeight policy satisfies the following upper bound on the expected total queue length:
\begin{align}\label{ieq:upp_bound_maxweight}
   \mathbb{E}\Big[\sum_{i=1}^n Q_i(T)\Big]\leq 
   n\sqrt{(B^2+C^2)(T-1)}+\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[A_i(T-1)].
 \end{align}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Recall \eqref{equ:f_drift+quadratic}. The MaxWeight policy minimizes the first-order term and ensures that this term remains non-positive at each step, since $\lambda(t) \in \Pi$ and, by definition of $\Pi$, $D^{\text{MaxWeight}}(t)$ maximizes $\langle Q(t), d \rangle$ over all $d \in \Pi$. Besides,
    the second-order term is bounded by $n B^2$.
    Substituting into \eqref{ieq:upp_bound}, we obtain~\eqref{ieq:upp_bound_maxweight}.
\end{proof}
The proof of the upper bound of the MaxWeight policy in Theorem \ref{thm:upper_bound_maxweight} highlights  a  key limitation of MaxWeight--and drift-based methods more broadly--which optimize only the first-order term. In particular, MaxWeight always selects extreme points and fails to adapt to the geometry of the arrival rate vector reflected in the second-order term, potentially leading to queue accumulation.

