\begin{abstract}
The rapid development of Artificial Intelligence Generated Content (AIGC) has made high-fidelity generated audio widely available across the Internet, providing diverse cover signals for covert communication. Driven by advances in deep learning, current audio steganography schemes are mainly based on encoding-decoding network architectures. While these methods greatly improve the security of audio steganography, they typically require complex training and large pre-trained models. To address the aforementioned issues, this paper pioneers a Fixed Decoder Network-Based Audio Steganography with Adversarial Perturbation Generation (FGAS). Adversarial perturbations carrying secret message are embedded into the cover audio to generate stego audio. The receiver only needs to share the structure and weights of the fixed decoder network to accurately extract the secret message from the stego audio, this eliminates the reliance on large pre-trained models. In FGAS, we propose an audio Adversarial Perturbation Generation (APG) strategy and design a lightweight fixed decoder. The fixed decoder guarantees reliable extraction of the hidden message, while the adversarial perturbations are optimized to keep the stego audio perceptually and statistically close to the cover audio, thereby improving resistance to steganalysis. The experimental results show that FGAS significantly improves the quality of stego audio, achieving an average PSNR gain of over 10 dB compared to SOTA methods. Moreover, FGAS exhibits superior anti-steganalysis performance under
different relative payloads; under high-capacity embedding, it achieves a classification error rate ($\overline{P}_{E}$) about 2\% higher, indicating stronger anti-steganalysis performance compared to current SOTA methods.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Audio steganography, Fixed decoder network, Adversarial perturbation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart {S}{teganography} is a critical information hiding technique that enables covert communication and protects individual privacy by embedding data within digital carriers such as images, audio, and other multimedia signals \cite{hu2025mutual,26,pan2025rethinking,cheng2025robust,hu2024establishing,meng2023robust}. In recent years, the development of generative models has dramatically changed the data environment for steganography \cite{zeng2024toward}. With the widespread use of high-fidelity generated audio online, which is the ideal cover for steganography, audio steganography has received more attention and warrants further investigation.\\
\indent Traditional audio steganography techniques\cite{5,6,7,8,9,10}, which typically operate in the time domain, rely on handcrafted embedding rules and manually tuned parameters to ensure imperceptible information embedding within cover signals. However, these approaches often require extensive tuning to maintain imperceptibility, and the imperceptibility of the stego audio tends to degrade significantly as the embedding payloads increase, making them vulnerable to steganalyzers. As a result, Deep Neural Network (DNN) based audio steganography \cite{11,12,13,14,15,30} has emerged, utilizing sophisticated neural architectures to enhance imperceptibility and anti-steganalysis performance \cite{29}. However, such methods present significant practical challenges. Their training pipelines are complex and computationally intensive, necessitating extensive pretraining and large pre-trained models, which consume substantial time and computational resources. Moreover, the transmission of voluminous pre-trained decoder models across social networks may raise suspicion from adversaries, ultimately undermining the security of the covert communication.\\
\textbf{\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=8.7cm]{AFNNS.pdf}
\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{0pt} 
\caption{Overview of the proposed FGAS framework. Secret message is embedded via adversarial perturbations and decoded using a shared fixed decoder.}
\label{fig:1}
\end{figure}}
\indent The contemporary challenge in the domain of audio steganography is to attain both steganographic imperceptibility and anti-steganalysis performance without the necessity to train or transmit large-scale pre-trained networks for extended periods of time. Leveraging fixed neural network developed in the image domain \cite{16,17,18,19,22} and informed by a comprehensive analysis of time-domain audio characteristics, we propose Fixed Decoder Network-Based Audio Steganography with Adversarial Perturbation Generation (FGAS) in the field of generative audio steganography. Specifically, the sender (Alice) employs a fixed decoder network to embed secret message into a cover audio, producing a stego audio that conceals the secret message. The receiver (Bob) then applies the same fixed decoder network to recover the secret message from the stego audio. The basic FGAS workflow is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1}. However, unlike image-based approaches that rely on two-dimensional spatial redundancy and visual perceptual masking to embed perturbations imperceptibly, audio steganography faces fundamentally different challenges. Raw audio signals are one-dimensional waveforms with high sampling rates, where even minimal perturbations in the time domain can lead to perceptible distortions. Additionally, the human auditory system is highly sensitive to subtle discontinuities and irregular noise in specific frequency ranges, rendering visual-domain masking strategies ineffective in the audio context. To address these challenges and ensure both anti-steganalysis and auditory imperceptibility, FGAS integrates adversarial training with stringent signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) constraints, ensuring that the perturbations remain below the auditory detection threshold.\\
\indent To address the inherent characteristics of audio mentioned above, we design a perturbation-sensitive fixed decoder network for audio to ensure that a wider temporal context is captured while stabilizing the feature distribution across the entire timeline, thus enabling reliable embedding and accurate extraction of secret message. While keeping the decoder network parameters fixed, the cover audio is modified via adversarial perturbations \cite{20} so that the stego audio triggers the decoder network to output the secret data. Meanwhile, we propose an audio Adversarial Perturbation Generation (APG) strategy to guide the direction of the generation of the adversarial perturbation, so that the stego audio is closer to the cover audio without hidden information under the discrimination of the steganalyzers. In addition, the generated adversarial perturbations are strictly size-constrained to minimize the difference between the cover audio and the stego audio, thereby ensuring audio quality. In practice, the shared key is used to initialize the weights of the decoder network, allowing a reliable extraction of the secret message.\\
\indent  We design a large number of experiments to comprehensively evaluate the performance of FGAS, including the anti-steganalysis performance, accuracy of secret message extraction, and the quality of stego audio. The results show that FGAS outperforms SOTA methods in all these aspects. In terms of anti-steganalysis performance, FGAS consistently maintains near-random-guess steganalyzer detection correctness over almost all relative payloads, while competing approaches experience a more pronounced degradation in anti-steganalysis performance at high relative payloads. In addition, FGAS guarantees the reliable recovery of secret messages with a bit error rate (BER) of no more than 1\% when the receiver is in possession of the correct key. In the case where the attacker does not have the correct key, the BER of recovering the secret message using the wrong key is about 50\%, which makes it impossible to obtain a valid secret message. The average Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) of stego audio generated by FGAS is typically more than 10 dB higher than that of competing methods across a wide range of datasets. These results described above confirm the practical value of FGAS for covert communication.\\
\indent Our main contributions are summarized below:
\begin{itemize}[label={•},labelindent=2em,leftmargin=*,itemindent=0em]
    \item Fixed Decoder Network-Based Audio Steganography with Adversarial Perturbation Generation (FGAS) is the first audio steganography scheme to employ a fixed decoder: the sender and receiver share the network architecture and initialization weights to embed and extract imperceptible secret-bearing audio perturbations, thereby eliminating the need for large pre-trained models and dramatically reducing both training and storage overhead.
    \item An audio Adversarial Perturbation Generation (APG) strategy is designed to guide the direction of the generation of the adversarial perturbation, so that the stego audio is closer to the cover audio under the discrimination of the steganalyzers.
    \item Extensive experiments show that, compared to other SOTA audio steganography scheme, our approach achieves superior stego audio quality, high secret message recovery accuracy, and significantly improved anti-steganalysis performance. Even at high relative payloads, the stego audio maintains minimal auditory distortion and remains undetected by advanced steganalyzers. 
\end{itemize}

 The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II provides a brief overview of the traditional and DNN-based audio steganography in time-domain and image steganography with application of fixed neural network approach. Section III describes the proposed steganography scheme, including the APG strategy and the perturbation-sensitive fixed decoder network design. The experimental results and analysis are given in Section IV. Finally, Section V gives the conclusion.\\
\section{Related Work}
\indent In this section, we divide existing audio steganography into two categories, traditional audio steganography and DNN-based audio steganography. In addition, the application and development of fixed neural network is investigated in steganography.
\subsection{Traditional Audio Steganography}
\indent Traditional audio steganography typically relies on meticulously engineered hand-crafted embedding schemes that introduce imperceptible modifications into the cover audio, thereby embedding secret data while maximally preserving perceptual quality. For example, some methods improve the least significant bit (LSB) hiding. Roy et al. \cite{5} designed a high bit rate LSB audio watermarking method that reduces embedding distortion of cover audio with increased capacity of secret text. In the study by Vimal et al. \cite{6} Dual Randomness LSB method is proposed which hides the secret message in randomly selected samples and in randomly selected bit. Mahmoud et al. \cite{7} proposed an enhanced LSB audio steganography based on Binaries of Message Size Encoding (BMSE).  It surpasses previous methods in hiding capacity and imperceptibility, but is less robust to noise and LSB attacks. These methods focus on the quality of the embedded payload and the quality of stego audio, and their detection performance against advanced steganalyzers remains unsatisfactory.\\
\indent Luo et al. \cite{8} proposed an adaptive audio steganography based on the advanced audio coding (AAC) and the Syndrome-Trellis coding (STC) \cite{9} to enhance security and audio quality. The method compresses the audio signal into an AAC file, calculates the residuals between the original and compressed signals, assigns ±1 embedding costs based on the characteristics of the residuals, and finally generates stego audio using STC. Su et al. \cite{10} proposed an audio steganography scheme combining Micro-Amplitude Suppression (MAS) and Generalized Audio Intrinsic Energy (GAIE) to enhance security by minimizing micro-amplitude variations and stabilizing adaptive embedding costs. This approach provides a new paradigm for hand-crafted audio steganography schemes.\\
\indent Although requiring extensive manual calibration to preserve imperceptibility, these techniques typically exhibit diminishing stealth capabilities as payload sizes increase, thus becoming more susceptible to contemporary steganalyzers \cite{28}.\\
\subsection{DNN-Based Audio Steganography}
\indent Due to the rapid development of deep learning in recent years, besides the hand-crafted audio steganography mentioned above, some deep learning based methods have also been proposed. For instance, Chen et al. \cite{30} proposed a carrier reproducible steganography approach grounded in a deep generative model, which leverages text-to-speech and image generation techniques to produce steganographic carriers. Yang et al. \cite{11} proposed a Generative Adversarial Network-based scheme to automatically learn the adaptive embedding probabilities for audio steganography. Inspired by the phenomenon that CNN-based classifiers are vulnerable to adversarial examples, Wu et al. \cite{12} proposed an Iterative Adversarial Attacks-based (IAA) scheme to improve the anti-steganalysis performance of steganographic methods. Gelet et al. \cite{13} presented PixInWav, a residual steganography method that hides images in audio using short-time discrete cosine transform (STDCT) spectrograms with independent encoding. Despite simultaneously achieving solid security performance, these schemes require a large amount of data and computational resources to train well-performing steganographic networks. In addition, in order to enhance the statistical security of steganography and resistance to steganalysis, among digital steganography, there are distribution-preserving stego systems \cite{14}  that utilize text-to-speech generative models and coverless audio steganography \cite{15} by generating carriers and establishing mapping rules rather than embedding data directly. While these novel approaches also deliver substantial gains in imperceptibility and security, they nonetheless rely on extensive training and large pre-trained architectures.
\subsection{Fixed Neural Network-based Steganography}
\indent To avoid training and transmitting the steganographic networks, fixed neural network emerged, which abandons the learned encoding network and conducts data hiding and recovery using a fixed decoder network. Currently, the application of fixed neural network in the field of images is well established. Kishore et al. \cite{16} introduced a method that enhances embedding capacity by increasing the output dimension of the decoder network and employs information loss to produce encrypted images. Li et al. \cite{18} introduced the Cover-separable Fixed Neural Network Steganography (Cs-FNNS) method, which integrates secret message with adversarial perturbations and uses a steganographic perturbation search algorithm to optimize the perturbations. \\
\indent Fixed neural network has gained application in the field of image processing. However, it has yet to be used in the field of audio steganography. Unlike image-based fixed neural network-based schemes, which exploit the inherent two-dimensional spatial redundancy and perceptual masking between pixels to disperse perturbations imperceptibly, raw audio consists of a one-dimensional, high-sampling-rate waveform in which even the slightest time-domain perturbations can produce audible artifacts. Moreover, the human auditory system is exquisitely sensitive to discontinuities and anomalous noise in certain frequency bands, meaning that traditional image-masking strategies based on visual perception cannot be applied directly. To achieve both imperceptibility and anti-steganalysis performance in the audio domain, adversarial training must be coupled with strict signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) constraints, ensuring that any embedded perturbations remain below the threshold of human hearing.\\
