\indent This section begins with an overview of the proposed framework, followed by a detailed explanation of the audio Adversarial Perturbation Generation (APG) strategy. Finally, we describe the design of the decoder network.
\subsection{Framework of the Proposed Scheme}
\indent In this study, the proposed audio steganography scheme is based on a fixed neural network architecture. Let $A_c$ denote lossless cover audio in WAV format, with $T$ and $H$ denoting its number of channels and samples, respectively. The secret message to be transmitted, denoted as $M$, is a randomly generated binary bit string of length $L$. For ease of description, the relevant symbols are shown in Table~\ref{tab:1}.\\
\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Notations}
\label{tab:1}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Notations & Description \\
\midrule
$A_c$ & Cover Audio $A_c \in [0,1]^{T \times H}$ \\
$A_s$ & Stego Audio $A_s \in [0,1]^{T \times H}$ \\
$\delta$ & Micro Perturbation $\delta \in [-\varepsilon, \varepsilon]^{T \times H}$ \\
$M$ & Secret Message $M \in \{0,1\}^L$ \\
$\hat{M}$ & Recovered Message $\hat{M} \in [0,1]^L$ \\
$D_e(\cdot)$ & Decoder Network $D_e: [0,1]^{T \times H} \to [0,1]^L$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\indent According to the framework depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}, on the sender side, for a given cover audio $A_c$ and secret message $M$, Alice embeds a tiny perturbation $\delta$ of strictly limited size, which can be reduced to the true secret message by a fixed neural network decoder, into the cover audio. This perturbation is iteratively optimized by audio adversarial perturbation generation strategy to counteract modern steganalysis detection and to be closer to the cover audio in auditory perception. In this way, when propagated over the public channel, stego audio is well hidden so that it remains indistinguishable to human perception and undetectable to steganalyzers. The weights of the decoder are initialized and stored as a key $k_r$.\\
\indent On the receiver side, Bob can recover the secret message $\hat{M}$ from the stego audio $A_s$ obtained in the public channel by sharing the key $k_r$ and the same fixed decoder network structure. Even if an attacker eavesdrops on the channel to intercept the stego audio, without knowing the exact key $k_r$, he cannot extract the secret message with the wrong initialization weights $k_w$. This process can be formally described as:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:1}
D_e[k_r](\delta)=\hat{M}.
\end{equation}
\subsection{Audio Adversarial Perturbation Generation strategy}
\indent In the context of propagating on public channels, it is imperative to consider the potential for attackers with ulterior motives to detect the intercepted stego audio using advanced steganalyzers and attempt to extract the secret information. In this scenario, the concealment of the stego audio and its anti-steganalysis performance are particularly important. To address the aforementioned issues, an audio adversarial perturbation generation (APG) strategy is proposed. APG strategy aims to find a perturbation $\delta \in [-\epsilon, \epsilon]^{C \times T}$ that satisfies the following three properties: 1) Minimizing the difference between cover audio $A_c$ and stego audio $A_s$ to ensure auditory covert; 2) ensuring that fixed decoder network $D_e(\cdot)$ can decode secret messages $M$; 3) maximizing the use of steganalysis to misclassify stego audio as cover audio. The optimization problem mentioned above can be expressed by the following mathematical formulation:\\
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2}
\left.\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\left\{
\begin{array}
{l}\|A_c-A_s\|\leq\epsilon\\
D_e[k_r](\delta)=\hat{M} \\
J(A_s)=0\\
-1\leq{A_s}\leq1
\end{array}\right.\right.,
\end{equation}
where the first condition strictly limits the size of the perturbation $\delta$ to ensure that it is small and stealthy. $J(\cdot)$ is a set of deep learning-based steganalyzers \cite{23,24}, each of which accepts an arbitrary audio and output the probability that the audio contains secret message. The final constraint ensures that the generated stego audio conforms to an acceptable amplitude range after normalization.\\
\indent We use different loss functions to optimize the performance of each part of the perturbation separately, so that the best generated perturbation $\delta$ can satisfy the constraints of the above formulation \\
\indent Correspondingly, to satisfy the first constraint above and mitigate the impact of perturbation on the quality of the cover audio, the perturbation introduced during the embedding process should be as minimal as possible. We use a loss function as follows:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:3}
L_1=MSELoss(As,Ac).
\end{equation}
$L_1$ measuring the difference between stego audio and cover audio. Specifically, to constrain the perturbation within the limits, we use $\epsilon$ to bound $\delta$, as shown in the following Eq.~\ref{eq:4}.
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:4}
-\epsilon\leq\delta\leq \epsilon\\
\end{equation}
\indent In addition to maintaining the quality of stego audio, it is critical to reliably extract secret message. In order to ensure the accuracy of the recovery information and to satisfy the second constraint, the binary cross entropy (BCE) loss function is introduced:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:5}
L_2=BCELoss(M,\hat{M}).
\end{equation}
$L_2$ measures the difference by calculating the cross entropy between the extracted bits of secret message and the original secret message, to maximize the precision of the recovery of secret message.\\
\indent To actively fool the steganalyzers, satisfy the third condition in the optimization task, we construct a paired input batch:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:6}
Z = \begin{bmatrix} A_c, A_s \end{bmatrix}, \quad Y = [0, 1]^\top,
\end{equation}
where label “0” denotes the cover signal $A_c$ and “1” denotes the stego signal $A_s$. We incorporate
pre-trained steganalyzers \cite{23,24} into the later iterations to provide gradient feedback for perturbation refinement, thereby enhancing the anti-steganalysis performance of the generated stego audio.\\
\indent Let the raw outputs (logits) from steganalyzers for the input batches $Z = \begin{bmatrix} A_c, A_s \end{bmatrix}$ be $s(Z)=[s_0,s_1]^\top$, and the corresponding predicted probabilities $p_j$ (Softmax) be:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:7}
p_j=\frac{\exp(s_j)}{\exp(s_0)+\exp(s_1)},\quad j\in\{0,1\}.
\end{equation}
\indent We then introduced define the adversarial detection loss:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:8}
L_3=CrossEntropyLoss(Z,Y),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:9}
CrossEntropyLoss= -\sum_{j=0}^1 Y_j \log \left( \frac{e^{s_j}}{e^{s_0} + e^{s_1}} \right).
\end{equation}
$L_3$ measures the discrepancy between the predicted probability distribution and the ground-truth label distribution, with the objective of misleading the steganalyzer into misclassifying stego audio as cover.\\
\indent On this basis, we now focus on the following three objectives: the quality of the stego audio, the correctness of the recovered secret bits, and the anti-steganalysis performance. Therefore, we can define the optimization problem as the following objective function $f(\delta)$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:10}
L=\alpha{L_1}+\beta{L_2}+\gamma{L_3},
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ are hyperparameters that balance the contributions of different loss functions.\\
