\subsection{Fixed Decoder Network}
\indent Architecture and weights are two essential elements in constructing the decoder network. Steganography for the application of fixed decoder networks in the image domain \cite{16,17,18,19} has demonstrated the sensitivity of decoding efficacy to architectural choices. The proposed audio decoder network retains the architectural motifs of the fixed image decoder network, namely convolutional layers, instance normalization and LeakyReLU activations with a final sigmoid output, but is restructured for one‐dimensional audio signals and enhanced in several key respects.\\
\indent Our decoder consists of five sequential Conv1d blocks. The first two Conv1d layers employ a larger kernel size of 5 to capture broader temporal contexts, while the remaining three use a kernel size of 3 to refine local detail. Each convolution is immediately followed by InstanceNorm1d, which stabilizes feature distributions across the temporal axis, and a LeakyReLU for robust non‐linear mapping. To guarantee that the recovered bit‐sequence matches the desired length, we introduce an adaptive pooling layer set to the target length prior to removing the singleton channel dimension and applying the final Sigmoid activation, as
illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:3}.\\
\indent After determining the design of the decoder network $D_e(\cdot)$ using this method and employing the shared key $k_r$ , both the sender and receiver can independently construct identical decoder network. This method significantly reduces he amount of information exchange required, thus enhancing both the imperceptibility and the anti-steganalysis performance of the steganographic system.\\
