\section{Experiment}
\subsection{Experiment Setups}
\footnotetext[1]{https://catalog.ldc.upenn.edu/}
\footnotetext[2]{https://keithito.com/LJ-Speech-Dataset/}
\subsubsection{\bf{Datasets}} The experiments in this section are performed on the TIMIT\textsuperscript{1} and LJSpeech\textsuperscript{2} datasets. We collected 3000 mono, 16-kHz, 16-bit cover speech clips in wav format, from TIMIT and LJSpeech, respectively, as cover audio. In order to unify the input format, we crop all audio to 1 second length (i.e., 16,000 samples) and normalize it to ensure that the amplitude range of the input audio is within [-1, 1].\\
\subsubsection{\bf{Optimization}} In the optimisation process, we used the Adam optimiser \cite{25} to minimise the loss function. The total number of training iterations was set to 2000, with an initial learning rate of 0.001. The well-trained ChenNet \cite{23} and LinNet \cite{24} steganographic network is used to provide gradient signals for our optimization. A random seed is used to initialise the fixed decoder network weights and saved as a key $k_r$.\\
\subsubsection{\bf{Evaluation}} Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) is used to measure the audible quality of the stego audios. The higher the PSNR value, the better the audio quality. The Classification Error Rate ($\overline{P}_{E}$) is used to evaluate the anti-steganalysis performance of our steganography scheme against the steganalyzer. Higher $\overline{P}_{E}$ values indicate better anti-steganalysis performance, reflecting greater stego audio evading detection, showing reduced distinguishability and detection confidence.\\
\subsubsection{\bf{Hyperparameters}} In the experiment, the hide loss weight $\alpha$ is set to 1, the recovery loss weight $\beta$ is set to 0.5, and the anti-steganalysis loss weight $\gamma$ is set to 0 for the first 1,500 iterations and dynamically increases to 0.01 for the last 500 iterations. \\
 \indent We investigate how varying the $\epsilon$ value affects PSNR, detection accuracy, and message recovery performance with other hyperparameters fixed. Experiments reveal that the audio quality index PSNR value will increase as the $\epsilon$ value becomes smaller, and when the $\epsilon$ value is less than 8e-3, the PSNR value will be greater than 100dB, which ensures good listening quality. The variation of the steganalyzer detection accuracy and secret message recovery accuracy for different $\epsilon$ values is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:4} and finally determined $0.001$ as the value of $\epsilon$.\\
