\subsection{ Stego Audio Quality}
\indent Stego audio quality not only governs the imperceptibility of the embedded payload, but also serves as a crucial metric for steganography’s anti-steganalysis performance. The quality of stego audio generated by our proposed method is quantitatively evaluated in Table~\ref{tab:2}, where mean PSNR values are reported across two benchmark datasets: TIMIT and LJSpeech, using two different evaluation networks: ChenNet and LinNet. Compared with the traditional steganography baselines, our method achieves significantly higher PSNR values under all conditions, indicating a substantial improvement in stego audio quality after data embedding.\\
\indent Table~\ref{tab:2} reports PSNR values for GAIE\_MAS \cite{10} and FGAS under relative payloads of 0.1 to 1 bps on TIMIT and LJSpeech. On TIMIT, GAIE\_MAS achieves 99.69 dB at a payload of 0.1 bps and decreases to 94.71 dB at 1 bps. In contrast, FGAS maintains PSNR above 107 dB, starting at 108.59 dB at 0.1 bps and ending at 107.39 dB at 1 bps, yielding a consistent improvement of around 10 dB. On LJSpeech, GAIE\_MAS starts at 98.71 dB at 0.1 bps and declines to 97.11 dB at 0.5 bps before falling to 93.20 dB at 1 bps. By comparison, FGAS begins at 108.32 dB at 0.1 bps and only drops to 106.44 dB at 1 bps, providing an advantage exceeding 10 dB up to 0.5 bps and approximately 13 dB at 1 bps. These results demonstrate that FGAS preserves audio fidelity more effectively under increasing payloads, indicating superior imperceptibility and enhanced robustness against steganalysis relative to GAIE\_MAS.\\
\indent Our approach achieves superior stego audio quality mainly because of the elaborate loss function mechanism used in the embedding process, which incorporates steganographic losses to control the magnitude of the perturbations and strictly limits the upper and lower bounds of the perturbations through the  $\epsilon$ parameter, as well as smoothing constraints using the tanh function, which ensures that the added perturbations are very small and uniformly distributed, rendering the stego audio almost audibly indistinguishable from the cover audio.
