\subsection{Anti-Steganalysis Performance}
\indent To comprehensively evaluate the anti-steganalysis performance of FGAS, we conduct experiments under different relative payloads and assess the performance using two publicly available audio steganalyzers, ChenNet \cite{23} and LinNet \cite{24}. This allows us to analyze and compare the the anti-steganalysis performance of each method across various payload levels. In particular, for the FGAS method, we also tested its anti-steganalysis performance at different values of $\epsilon$. The results of these methods on the TIMIT and LJSpeech dataset are shown in Table~\ref{tab:3}.\\
\indent Across all relative payloads ranges from 0.1 bps to 1.0 bps, our method maximizes the classification error rate ($\overline{P}_{E}$) at $\epsilon=1e-3$, bringing the steganalysis accuracy of ChenNet and LinNet close to random guessing. Compared to the GAIE\_MAS \cite{10} method, our method has higher values of $\overline{P}_{E}$ on both datasets at $\epsilon=1e-3$, with less decline at high relative payloads. This demonstrates that FGAS is well adapted to different audio content and statistical properties.\\
\indent The above experimental results are from the adversarial training mechanism introduced by FGAS. Specifically, adding a specialised anti-steganalysis loss term at a later stage of the training process and balancing the steganographic quality and anti-detection performance with the $\gamma$ parameter, which makes it difficult for the steganalyzers to accurately determine whether the audio contains steganographic information or not by allowing the stego audio to actively fight against the state-of-the-art steganalyzers.\\
\indent To further evaluate the generalization capability of the proposed FGAS scheme under unseen steganalyzer conditions, we introduce a variant called FGAS*. Unlike the original FGAS setup, which leverages a specific steganalyzer both during adversarial perturbation generation and evaluation, FGAS* employs a mismatched steganalyzer setup: perturbations are generated using one steganalyzer and then evaluated using a different one. This simulates real-world scenarios where the steganalyzer architecture is unknown at embedding time, and allows us to assess the transferability of the generated perturbations across detection models.\\
\indent The results, also presented in Table~\ref{tab:3}, demonstrate that \textbf{FGAS*} maintains high $\overline{P}_{E}$ values during the evaluation of the cross-model, closely matching the performance of the standard FGAS. This suggests that the learned perturbations are highly transferable and are not overfitted to a specific steganalysis model, thus confirming the practical applicability of FGAS under unknown detection environments.\\
\subsection{Secret Message Extraction}
\indent To evaluate our fixed decoder network, we performed experiments across relative payloads from 0.1 bps to 1 bps using both the correct initialization key and an incorrect key. In our design, the secret key serves to initialize the decoder’s weights; by fixing this randomly seeded initialization at both sender and receiver, we eliminate the need to transmit a large pretrained model while ensuring that only a recipient possessing the exact key can recover the hidden bits. Table~\ref{tab:4} reports the mean bit recovery accuracy for each payload when decoding with the correct key versus a wrong key.\\
\indent When decoding with the correct key, bit recovery accuracy exceeds 99.5\% for payloads up to 0.5 bps and remains above 99\% even at 1 bps. This near‐perfect performance demonstrates that our fixed‐weight decoder, seeded by the shared key, faithfully extracts embedded bits across all tested embedding rates. In contrast, initializing with an incorrect key yields a random weight configuration that cannot interpret the stego perturbation; recovery accuracy in this case hovers around 50\% (random guessing) at every payload. In other words, without the exact key, an adversary who knows the network architecture but not the seed is incapable of extracting any meaningful information from the stego audio.\\
\indent By using the secret key to fix decoder weights rather than exchanging a full model, our framework achieves two major advantages. First, it guarantees high-fidelity bit recovery for any recipient who holds the correct key, with BER consistently below 1\% under typical payloads. Second,  an unauthorized party lacking the key will only recover random bits, effectively preventing any successful extraction of confidential information. Moreover, because only the network architecture and a small random seed (the key) must be shared, our approach minimizes communication overhead while maximizing both imperceptibility and anti-steganalysis performance.\\
