\subsection{Ablation Studies}
\indent In order to investigate the extent to which the anti-steganalysis module in FGAS contributes to the anti-steganalysis performance and quality of the generated stego audio, we conducted ablation experiments. 
\subsubsection{Steganalysis}
\indent The anti-steganalysis module is an important part of the APG strategy. With the intention of actively deceiving the steganalyzers, we proceed to embed pre-trained steganalyzers \cite{23,24} in the latter part of the iterative process. They function to supply gradient feedback for the refinement of perturbation, which consequently bolsters the anti-steganalysis performance of the generated stego audio. We evaluate the anti-steganalysis performance $\overline{P}_{E}$ values of stego audio generated on the two datasets against Chennet and Linnet steganalysis with and without the anti-steganalysis module, at different relative payloads. The results are shown in the $\overline{P}_{E}$ row Table~\ref{tab:5}.\\
\indent It can be seen that the anti-steganalysis performance $\overline{P}_{E}$ value will decrease significantly after the anti-steganalysis module is removed, indicating that our anti-steganalysis module is able to effectively guide the generated stego audio to achieve a superior anti-steganalysis Performance.\\
\indent In addition, we plotted the time-domain waveforms, frequency-domain waveforms, and energy maps of the cover audio, the stego audio, and the perturbations generated with and without the anti-steganalysis module to show the comparison of their performances in terms of anti-steganalysis \cite{31,32,33}. The results are shown in Fig.~ \ref{fig:4},~\ref{fig:5},~\ref{fig:6}, respectively. It can be seen that the perturbations generated with the anti-steganalysis module are more uniformly distributed in the frequency domain, with a more natural energy distribution and lower overall energy than the perturbations generated without the anti-steganalysis module. 
\subsubsection{Audio Quality}
\indent As shown in the PSNR row of Table~\ref{tab:6}, under low relative payloads (0.1–0.4 bps), stego audio produced with the anti‑steganalysis module achieves higher PSNR values than that generated without the module. Conversely, at higher relative payloads (0.5–1 bps), the PSNR values for stego audio with the anti‑steganalysis module are slightly lower than those without the module, although the difference is negligible.\\
\indent This is because, under low relative payload conditions, the amount of information to be embedded is minimal, requiring only slight modifications to the audio. The anti‑steganalysis module therefore drives the system to produce more natural perturbations that avoid conspicuous statistical artifacts, which in turn leads to more dispersed and uniform distortions. Moreover, the reduced embedding capacity grants the system greater optimization flexibility, enabling it to strike an optimal balance between steganographic quality and detection resistance. \\
\indent While high relative payloads require embedding more information, which in turn demand more substantial modifications to the audio. The anti‑steganalysis module must therefore juggle three objectives simultaneously: minimizing steganographic perturbations, maximizing extraction accuracy, and maximizing anti-steganalysis performance. These goals inherently conflict, especially under high‑capacity conditions: accurately embedding a larger payload necessitates more pronounced alterations, yet such conspicuous changes are more readily detected, undermining anti-steganalysis performance. As a result, the system is forced to make trade‑offs among these objectives. Without the anti‑steganalysis module, only two objectives need to be optimized, and in the absence of steganalysis pressure, the system can concentrate more fully on preserving audio quality.\\
\section{Conclusion}
\indent In this paper, we propose a Fixed Decoder Network-Based Audio Steganography with Adversarial Perturbation Generation (FGAS) that combines the generated adversarial perturbations carrying secret message with cover audio to produce stego audio. The produced audio Adversarial Perturbation Generation (APG) strategy ensures accurate decoding while making the stego audio closer to the cover audio under the discrimination of the steganalyzers. The method generates high-quality stego audio under different relative payloads and exhibits superior anti-steganalysis performance, outperforming existing SOTA methods. \\

