\subsubsection{Related projects}
The linear and quadratic functions described above are the simplest of the so-called Lagrange-type shape functions and are the main focus of our paper. Other available implementations that use the vectorization library include:

\begin{itemize}
%    \item nodal linear shape functions \cite{} - explained and further developed in this contribution,
    \item edge shape functions on triangles or tetrahedra known as Raviart-Thomas and Nedelec elements \cite{AnjamValdman2015},
      \item nodal shape functions on rectangles ensuring the continuity of the first gradient along edges \cite{Valdman2020} known as Bogner–Fox–Schmitt element,
    \item nodal hierarchical-type functions on rectangles \cite{HP} allowing an arbitrary order of a polynomial shape function.
\end{itemize}

Another attempt to avoid the setup of sparse matrices and to work completely with a multidimensional array in terms of iterative solvers is documented in \cite{MarcinkowskiValdman2020}.

