\begin{abstract}

According to Yuval Noah Harari, large-scale human cooperation is driven by shared narratives that encode common beliefs and values. This study explores whether such narratives can similarly nudge LLM agents toward collaboration. 


We use a finitely repeated public goods game in which LLM agents choose either cooperative or egoistic spending strategies. We prime agents with stories highlighting teamwork to different degrees and test how this influences negotiation outcomes.


Our experiments explore four questions:
(1) How do narratives influence negotiation behavior?
(2) What differs when agents share the same story versus different ones?
(3) What happens when the agent numbers grow?
(4) Are agents resilient against self-serving negotiators?


We find that story-based priming significantly affects negotiation strategies and success rates. \emph{Common} stories improve collaboration, benefiting each agent. By contrast, priming agents with \emph{different} stories reverses this effect, and those agents primed toward self-interest prevail. We hypothesize that these results carry implications for multi-agent system design and AI alignment.

%Yuval Noah Harari suggests that large-scale human cooperation is driven by shared narratives that encode common beliefs and values. This study investigates whether similar narratives can influence collaboration among large language model (LLM) agents. Using a resource-allocation game, we prime agents with stories highlighting teamwork to varying degrees and observe the impact on their negotiation strategies and outcomes. The research explores the extent to which narratives influence negotiation behavior, examining how the effects change when all agents are exposed to the same story versus different ones. It also considers how collaboration dynamics shift as the number of agents increases and whether story-primed agents can maintain cooperative strategies in the presence of self-serving negotiators. The findings reveal that narrative priming significantly shapes negotiation strategies and success rates. When agents share a common narrative, cooperation is enhanced, benefiting all participants. In contrast, when agents are primed with different narratives, cooperation diminishes, and self-interested behavior prevails. These results offer insights into multi-agent system design and AI alignment, highlighting the role of narrative structures in influencing agent behavior and fostering collaboration.



Code is available at {\hyperlink{https://github.com/storyagents25/story-agents}{\small \texttt{github.com/storyagents25/story-agents}}}

\keywords{LLM Agents  \and Narrative Priming \and Collaboration and Competition.}
\end{abstract}

