\paragraph{\textbf{Game Theory \& Economics in LLM Negotiations.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \cite{meta2022human} introduce CICERO AI which achieved human-level perforomance in the Diplomacy game by integrating strategic planning with natural language negotiation. Diplomacy is a 7-player game of alliance formation and betrayal - a classic game-theoretic scenario requiring dependable commitments and timely cooperation. Cicero infers players' beliefs and intentions and negotiates the plans accordingly. 
    \item \cite{gemp2024steering} propose a framework integrating game-theoretic equilibrium solvers with LLMs to steer strategic language generation in dialogues (e.g., negotiation, debate). By modeling interactions as games with payoffs and strategies, solvers guide LLMs to generate less exploitable, higher-reward responses. Experiments across three domains show solver-guided LLMs outperform unguided baselines, demonstrating improved rationality. This bridges game theory’s symbolic reasoning with LLMs’ linguistic flexibility, enabling more principled multi-agent interactions. 
    \item \cite{fan2024can} investigate whether LLMs can be considered rational players in classical games (e.g. dictator game, rock-paper-scissors), providing a foundation for understanding agent behavior in strategic interactions. They systematically show LLMs often deviate from game-theoretic rationality and urge caution in substituting LLMs for humans in social science experiments due to their unresolved deficiencies in strategic reasoning. 
    \item \cite{sreedhar2024simulating} demonstrate that multi-agent systems replicate human strategic reasoning better (85\% accuracy) than single LLMs (50\%) in the ultimatum game, as single LLMs often fail to create consistent strategies. Results suggest multi-agent architectures better capture complex social dynamics (e.g. fairness enforcement), allowing policymakers to simulate interactions between agents with divergent goals or personalities. 
    \item Behavioral economics experiments also reveal factors that facilitate or hinder cooperation. \cite{meyer2021economic} ran a field experiment framing a pest-control task as a public goods game in rural communities. They found that playing a cooperative game significantly increased real-world collaboration among farmers, reducing crop losses by ~20\%. The key mechanism was changing villagers' beliefs about others' willingness to cooperate, echoing game-theoretic models where common knowledge of trust is critical. 
    \item From a broader perspective, Shiller \cite{shiller2020narrative} introduced a concept of "Narrative Economics", arguing that popular stories (e.g. boom/bust lore) spread contagiously and shape economic outcomes. This suggests taht beyond formal incentives, the narratives people believe (optimist, distrust, etc.) can alter bargaining power, market  behavior and cooperation. 
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{\textbf{Psychological \& Behavioral Studies on Cooperation.}}
\paragraph Psychology offers rich insights into how humans collaborate, which an inspire AI systems designed to wirk with or like people. Extensive research shows that humans are not purely self-interest maximizers; social context and mental framing strongly affect cooperative behavior. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \cite{van2022human} survey how evolutionary mechanisms (kinship, reciprocity, reputation) and cultural norms jointly determine human cooperation. They emphasize that successful cooperation in societal dilemmas requires leveraging both classical incentives and psychological factors (e.g. establishing reputations for honesty or invoking shared identities). 
    \item \cite{jung2020prosocial} conduct a meta-analysis on prosocial modeling, finding that observing someone perform a kind act significantly increases the likelihood that a witness will later help others. Simply seeing generosity "go viral" creates a goal contagion effect, where people adopt the implied norm of caring for others. This suggests that priming AI agents with examples of cooperation might similarly foster collaborative norms.
    \item \cite{mieth2021moral} demonstrate that adding moral language to a Prisoner's Dilemma - calling the choices "I cooperate" vs "I cheat" insead of neutral labels - activates a cooperative norm and substantially raise cooperation rates. Players even punished defectors more when moves were morally framed, indicating they internalized the narrative of right vs wrong. Such moral framing effects highlight the power of wording and could be used to prime AI or human partners towards fairness.
    \item \cite{henrich2021origins} argue that human ultra-sociality is a product of both genetic evolution and cultural evolution. Over time humans developed psychological adaptations (e.g. empathy, norm adherence) that, combined with cultural norms and institutions (religion, reputation systems), sustan large-scale cooperation. 

\end{itemize}

