This paper establishes the first results for the stable pair $H^1(\mathbb{S}^2) \times L^2(\mathbb{R}^2)$ based on the CG-DG pair, providing a foundation for designing stable pairs. Specifically, we show that the space $\div \Sigma_k^0$ can be explicitly characterized. Moreover, in the absence of nearly singular vertices (defined precisely later), the triple $(\Sigma_k^0, \div \Sigma_k^0)$ constitutes a stable pair, see \Cref{thm:lagrange}. In practice, several approaches can generate triangular meshes avoiding singular vertices. For example, a general triangular mesh can be refined using the Morgan-Scott split, while a general rectangular mesh can be transformed into a fish-bone mesh, as illustrated in \Cref{fig:fg-fb}.


