\subsection{Practical benefits}
In terms of practical benefits, the masking approach is relatively easier to use compared to the recurrent approach. We highlight several reasons why this is the case.
\textbf{(i) Software design.} The recurrent approach required signals to be passed \textit{backward} in time. This increased the complexity of software design and was not often intuitive for end users. With the masking approach, there is no need to reverse the signal.
\textbf{(ii) Static graph structure.} With the recurrent approach, the choice of time interval would fundamentally change the dimension of hidden states, whereas the graph structure remains the same for the masking approach.
Keeping the same graph structure even if the intervals change is essential in compilation.
\textbf{(iii) Vectorization and just-in-time compilation.} Related to the second point above, the static graph structure afforded by the masking approach enables the ability to easily \textit{vectorize} and \textit{just-in-time compile} (JIT) the computation over not just various signal inputs but also various time intervals. The ability to vectorize and JIT over different time intervals can be particularly useful in formula mining applications where we may want to simultaneously evaluate robustness formulas with different time intervals.
\textbf{(iv) Conformity to popular deep learning libraries.} By leveraging AD libraries that power popular deep learning frameworks (e.g., JAX and PyTorch), \stlcgpp\ becomes more accessible to the broader community and seamlessly integrates into the ecosystem.



\section{Computational properties of \stlcgpp}
%

In this section, we analyze the computational properties of the approaches \stlcgpp \ (masking-based) and \stlcg \ (recurrent-based) by measuring the computation time required to compute robustness values and their gradient. We seek to answer the following research questions.

\noindent \textbf{RQ1}: Does \stlcgpp\ compute robustness traces faster than \stlcg\ as measured by median computation time?

\noindent \textbf{RQ2}: How does \stlcgpp's computation time scale with sequence length compared to \stlcg?

We test on three different patterns of (temporal) STL formulas:  $\phi_1 = \Always (\phi \wedge \psi)$ (single temporal operator),  $\phi_2 = \Eventually \Always (\phi \wedge \psi)$ (two nested temporal operators), and $\phi_3 =\phi \, \Until \psi$ (the until operator), where $\phi$ and $\psi$ are simple predicates. These formulas were chosen as representatives of commonly encountered patterns in practice, as highlighted in \cite{HeBartocciEtAl2022}.

%

%

%



%
%
We evaluate computation time for increasing signal lengths up to $T=512$ time steps with a batch size of $8$. For context, trajectory lengths from robotics-related trajectory optimization problems are typically $\leq 50$.  We also consider both CPU and GPU backends.
We present our results in Fig.~\ref{fig:computation times} and Table~\ref{tab:computational improvement} using JAX with JIT compilation (JAX+JIT) and PyTorch and make the following observations.

%
\subsection{Evaluation with CPU backend}
\label{subsec:cpu}


\noindent \textbf{RQ1.} We observe that the masking approach achieves lower (median) computation times compared to the recurrent approach for both $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$. This trend is observed for both JAX+JIT and Pytorch. The masking-based approach performs worse than the recurrent-based approach for $\phi_3$ for JAX+JIT but performs better for Pytorch. We believe that this difference is observed due to how each library manages and allocates memory on CPU.

%

\noindent \textbf{RQ2.}
%
As expected, the computation time for both masking and recurrent approaches increases with the input length. However, while the time of the recurrent approach grows linearly due to its sequential processing, masking exhibits a slower increase in computation time for $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$. This makes masking more suitable for handling long sequence lengths for those formulas. However, for $\phi_3$ we observe that the masking approach performs significantly worse on JAX but outperforms on PyTorch for signal lengths $\leq$ 128.


%
%

%

%
%
\subsection{Evaluation with GPU backend}
\label{subsec:gpu}
\noindent \textbf{RQ1.} The masking approach performs significantly faster than the recurrent approach when using the GPU, for all test cases. This difference is statistically significant ($p<0.001$), indicating a clear advantage of the masking approach over the recurrent approach when using GPU.

\noindent \textbf{RQ2.} The computation time for both masking and recurrent approaches increases with sequence length. For $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ the recurrent approach scales linearly with a steep growth rate, while the masking approach remains nearly constant, demonstrating superior efficiency on GPU. For $\phi_3$, both approaches exhibit linear scaling.


\subsection{Summary discussion}
We observe that across all backends, AD libraries, and sequence lengths, masking outperforms the recurrent-based approach for formulas such as $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$. As highlighted in \cite{HeBartocciEtAl2022}, these formulas pattern occur the most in surveyed literature and are used extensively for various applications. For formulas with the Until operator, we observe that the choice of approach depends on the backend. For GPU backends, the masking-based approach is preferred over the recurrent approach. However, for the CPU backend, the masking approach performs worse as the 3D signal masking incurs significant memory costs.
