\subsection{Early Termination of Negative Computations}
Most CNN accelerator designs concentrate on efficiently generating the SOP for the activation layer (ReLU). However, few studies have investigated the early detection of negative values in the SOP, which presents a significant challenge in accelerators based on conventional arithmetic. For instance, conventional bit-serial multipliers take the multiplicand in parallel while processing the multiplier serially. In each iteration, a partial product is generated and stored in a register, then shifted into the appropriate position before being added to other partial products to compute the final result. This process typically involves a series of adders for reduction. A second level of reduction is necessary to add \( k \times k \) products to yield the output pixel, along with an additional level of reduction for summing multiple input channels. With conventional bit-serial multipliers, the most significant bit and the polarity of the result cannot be determined until all partial products have been generated and added to the previous partial sums.

The challenge of early detection and termination of negative activations can be addressed by the intrinsic ability of online arithmetic to generate output digits in an MSDF manner. The proposed design supports the termination of negative activation computation in $p<\mathcal{N}$ cycles, where $\mathcal{N}$ is the number of cycles to compute complete result. This is done by observing the output digits. The process of detecting the negative activations and subsequently terminating the relevant computation is summarized in Algorithm \ref{alg:ENT}.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Early detection and termination of negative activations}\label{alg:ENT}
\begin{algorithmic}
\State{$z^{+}_j,\ z^{-}_j \ $ bits} 
\For{$j = 1 \ to \ \mathcal{N}$}

        \State $z^{+}[j] \gets z^{+}[j]\ ^\frown z^{+}_{j}$\ %\Comment{$\frown$ Concat}
        \State $z^{-}[j] \gets  z^{-}[j]\ ^\frown z^{-}_{j}$\
        \If{$z^{+}[j] \ < \ z^{-}[j]$}
         \newline   Terminate

        \Else
         \newline   Continue
        \EndIf
      \EndFor    
\end{algorithmic}

\end{algorithm}

% The proposed early negative detection unit (END-U) is equipped with registers to store $z^{+}_j$ and $z^{-}_j$ bits, which are the positive and negative output bits of the SOP representing the output digits in redundant form. During each iteration, the new bits are appended in their corresponding registers. As soon as the value of $z^{+}[j]$ goes below the value of $z^{-}[j]$, a termination signal is generated resulting in the termination of the SOP computation. The END-U is integrated in each processing unit, described in Section \ref{sec:accelerator}.
\noindent The proposed early negative detection unit (END-U) is equipped with registers to store \( z^{+}_j \) and \( z^{-}_j \) bits, which represent the positive and negative output bits of the sum of products (SOP) in redundant form. During each iteration, new bits are appended to their respective registers. As soon as the value of \( z^{+}[j] \) falls below the value of \( z^{-}[j] \), a termination signal is generated, resulting in the cessation of the SOP computation. The END-U is integrated into each processing unit, as described in Section \ref{sec:accelerator}.

