\subsection*{Roots at $a$ or $b$}

Naturally, we don't want to consider functions that have a root between $a$ and $b$, since this doesn't make for a good physical model. However, there is no reason that we cannot create models for functions that have roots \emph{at} $a$ or $b$. To this end, we have created instructions that will instruct the crocheter to either begin or end a closed shape when appropriate. If there is a root at both $a$ and $b$, we further include instructions for stuffing the shape with fiberfill---a mathematical plushie!

