\opening{Dear Professors Gini, Miura, and Petrović:} 

I am pleased to submit the manuscript entitled ``Very Large-scale Multi-Robot Task Allocation in Challenging Environments via Robot Redistribution'', by Seabin Lee, Joonyeol Sim, and Changjoo Nam, to be considered for publication in \textit{Robotics and Autonomous Systems}. 

In this manuscript, we propose a scalable MRTA method that considers the paths of the robots to avoid collisions and deadlocks which result in a fast completion of all tasks. To incorporate robot paths into task allocation, the proposed method constructs a roadmap using a Generalized Voronoi Diagram. The method partitions the roadmap into several components to know how to redistribute robots to achieve all tasks with less conflicts between the robots.
In the redistribution process, robots are transferred to their final destinations according to a push-pop mechanism with the first-in first-out principle. From the extensive experiments, we show that our method can handle instances with hundreds of robots in dense clutter while competitors are unable to compute a solution within a time limit.

We believe that this manuscript is appropriate for publication in the journal because it provides theoretical and empirical contributions to autonomous multi-robot planning and coordination in complex environments. Unlike conventional approaches that optimize task allocation without considering inter-robot conflicts, our method explicitly accounts for robot paths, reducing deadlocks and congestion during task execution. By integrating path-aware allocation strategies and redistribution mechanisms, our approach enhances the efficiency of large robot teams (up to 500) operating in complex environments. We also provide a formal analysis for the proposed method.

