\noindent{\it Keywords}: neuromorphic engineering, olfaction, chemical sensing, synthetic biology
% \begin{indented}
%\item[]October 2017 (minor update March 2024)
% \end{indented}

\begin{abstract}
In this study, we explore how the combination of synthetic biology, neuroscience modeling, and neuromorphic electronic systems offers a new approach to creating an artificial system that mimics the natural sense of smell. We argue that a co-design approach offers significant advantages in replicating the complex dynamics of odor sensing and processing. We investigate a hybrid system of synthetic sensory neurons that provides three key features: a) receptor-gated ion channels, b) interface between synthetic biology and semiconductors and c) event-based encoding and computing based on spiking networks. This research seeks to develop a platform for ultra-sensitive, specific, and energy-efficient odor detection, with potential implications for environmental monitoring, medical diagnostics, and security.
%Neuromorphic computing, modeled after the architecture and functions of the human brain, holds great promise in replicating biological processes like vision, hearing, and smell. In this article, we explore how combining synthetic biology with neuromorphic electronic circuits offers a new approach in creating an artificial system that mimics the natural sense of smell.
%We argue that a co-design approach—where synthetic biological components, neuromorphic systems and computational models are developed together—offers significant advantages in replicating the complex dynamics of olfactory sensory processes.
%By integrating engineered olfactory receptors with advanced neuromorphic hardware, we aim to create a platform capable of highly sensitive, specific, and energy-efficient odor detection. These systems rely on multichannel decoding algorithms and event-driven spike-based computing to closely imitate the biological mechanisms and the signal processing pathways of natural olfaction. This interdisciplinary approach addresses the current challenges of artificial olfaction, including high power consumption, low sensitivity to complex odor mixtures, and lack of real-time processing. Our work has the potential to drive advances in applications like environmental monitoring, medical diagnostics, and security.
\end{abstract}

