\subsection{Tasks}

We sought software engineering tasks for this study that:

\begin{itemize}
    \item reflected developers' typical day-to-day tasks;
    \item participants were motivated to solve thoroughly;
    \item could be completed in less than an hour,
         enabling us to analyze end-to-end agent-participant collaboration; and
    \item would not use proprietary code, which participants may not be allowed
        to show us as researchers or to send to a third-party AI tool.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
% We aimed to foster a real-world environment that would reflect developers' typical day-to-day tasks
% foster a real-world environment in which participants were motivated to solve the tasks efficiently and accurately. Further, we aimed to use tasks that would reflect tasks that participant
% Participants worked on open issues (such as bugs or feature requests) in large open-source repositories that they were familiar with. 
% These issues were either listed on GitHub or tasks assigned to the participant.

% This fostered a realistic environment for the use of the tool in which participants were motivated to solve the tasks efficiently and accurately. 
Thus, we asked participants to work on recent open issues (such as bugs or feature requests) 
in open-source repositories that they had worked on before. We applied the following guidelines to select suitable repositories that were:
\begin{itemize}
    \item of significant size, that is, with more than 50 
          source code (i.e., non-documentation and non-configuration) files; 
          % \ysrt{Clarity: non-configuration?}
    \item actively maintained (i.e., had commits in the prior month);
    \item software centric, thus excluding repositories such as a collection of study materials or                                 tutorials~\cite{10.1145/3671016.3671401}; and
    \item starred over 500 times on GitHub, an 
          indicator of the maturity of the repository~\cite{sens2025largescalestudymodelintegration}.
\end{itemize}
\noindent
% \ysrt{table/figure better representation?} - wish we had space
Additionally, we selected repositories that were part of two major
GitHub organizations that are managed by our company. 
% \ysrt{Partial identification?} % -- might be but we probably have to characterize the issues in some way
This enabled us to select participants based
on internal-only information about repository contributors, like 
seniority and geographic location, and to communicate
more easily with those contributors.

% Based on our study description, some participants prepared tasks to work on ahead of time.
While most participants selected their first task to complete based on the list 
of open issues in their project,
some participants selected tasks to work on ahead of time. 
We further asked participants to choose issues that could be 
solved based on code edits, rather than configuration or documentation fixes, 
to ensure the agent could provide meaningful assistance. 

If participants had time to work on multiple tasks during the session, 
after they completed their first task,
we collaborated with them to choose subsequent issues 
to ensure diversity in: 

\begin{itemize}
    \item task type (e.g., if the first issue was a bug, 
    we encouraged participants to next select a feature request), and
    \item difficulty and scope (e.g., if the first issue was completed 
    easily, we encouraged participants to next select an issue that seemed more challenging).
\end{itemize}

