\subsubsection{Knowledge Transfer}\label{sec:expert}
% \ysrt{Non-PII Quotes could help visualize better these expert insights in this section}
When working with the agent to solve issues, we observed that participants provided two different types of information to the agent to help it make the desired code changes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item\textit{Contextual knowledge} from the issue description 
        and the environmental context (such as test logs). Eg: \textit{``There are build errors in your solution"}
    \item\textit{Expert knowledge} relating to code implementation or convention 
        that cannot be observed directly from context or the issue description 
        and is based on the developer's prior knowledge of the repository. Eg: \textit{``Usually we don't set an upperbound. I think 3.10 should be fine now"}
        % \ysrt{Should rename differently coz of overlapping `expert' in the sub-category?}
\end{itemize}

Participants' patterns of providing such knowledge to the agent mirror 
those of software engineering managers.
Kalliamvakou and colleagues~\cite{managerinse} report that when assigning work,
great managers in software engineering leave the implementation details 
to the engineer, providing actionable feedback when required. 
Analogously, participants tended to provide less expert 
knowledge to the agent up-front, and more as feedback;
participants provided expert knowledge in 49\% of prompts 
that asked the agent to generate a new code change, compared to 
in 65\% of prompts that ask the agent to refine a code change (i.e., iterate upon a previously generated change).
This relationship was even stronger for the 12 participants in 
managerial roles -- they provided expert knowledge in 45\% of their
prompts seeking new changes versus 75\% seeking to refine changes.
As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:perception},
the analogy is reflected in the post-study questionnaire, 
in which participants report viewing the agent more as an 
assistant (at a lower level in the organizational hierarchy) 
than as a colleague (at the same level) 
or advisor (at a higher level).

While participants usually tended to 
provided expert knowledge in feedback prompts rather than up-front, 
we found that experience factors moderated this relationship.
Participants who had more familiarity with the repository tended 
to provide expert knowledge more often -- participants with 
over 10 commits to the repository in the four months leading up to the 
study provided such insights 70\% of time when seeking any code changes 
as compared to 48\% for other participants. 
Similarly, participants who had previous experience with Cursor also 
provided more expert knowledge, doing so in 81\% of prompts seeking 
code changes. 
These participants also tended to provide expert knowledge more often as 
feedback -- they did so for 89\% of prompts trying to refine a 
code change as compared to for 67\% of prompts when asking for a new code change.  Further, participants applying the one-shot strategy provided expert knowledge less often (in 47\% of their prompts seeking code changes) as compared to those applying the incremental resolution strategy (did so in 64\% of their prompts seeking code changes).

