\subsubsection{Manual Actions} \label{manact}

Participants' perceptions of the agent shaped not only the types of 
prompts they provided the agent, 
but also the role the participants themselves took in the collaboration,
tending to work on verification (debugging and testing) more than on content generation (writing code).
% While agents have the capability to perform all parts of the software development process by analysing the codebase, making code changes and running terminal commands, participants often performed such actions manually as well, tending to work on debugging and testing more than on writing code while collaborating with the agent.
Since participants reported not thinking of the agent 
as a code reviewer (Figure~\ref{fig:perception}), 
they often took the responsibility to manually debug and test code upon themselves,
doing so in 21 out of 33 issues by running tests or examining execution logs. 
In the case of bugs concerning UI changes, 
it was also easier for the participant to test changes manually by 
simply observing the current state of the UI rather than uploading images to the chat for the agent to interpret.

On the other hand, since the participants tended to view
the agent more as a content generator, 
they were more likely to let the agent handle all the code 
changes to be made, manually writing code in only 14 issues. 
Further, in 10 of these issues, participants' code interventions 
were limited to editing code suggested by the agent 
rather than adding new functionality.
