\subsubsection{Reviewing Outputs} \label{sec:revoutput}

The agent provided output to communicate with participants in 
three ways:

\begin{itemize}
    \item\textit{Execution Trajectory}: a trace of the agent's work that appears
    while the agent is working, including context gathering steps such as codebase searches and files read (Figure ~\ref{fig:workflow}b), along with actions taken such as terminal commands executed and files edited.
    (the bulk of Figure~\ref{fig:workflow}).
    \item\textit{Change Explanation}: at the end of a trace, typically the agent summarized
    the change it made to the participant's codebase (Figure~\ref{fig:workflow}c).
    \item\textit{Change Diff}: inside the participant's editor, the agent provides
    a number of inline diffs as proposed changes that the participant can accept or reject (Figure~\ref{fig:additional}).
\end{itemize}

We observed that participants tended to first track the agent's execution live 
as-it-happened before reviewing explanations and diffs, 
with a preference for reading diffs. 
While Cursor enables its users to do manual tasks in parallel to its
own execution,  
participants instead followed the agent's execution as it 
happened in real time for 84\% of their prompts.
In some cases (23\% of prompts), this was sufficient validation for participants,
as they did not further verify the diffs or agent explanations.
Usually, this live analysis was at least sufficient for participants 
to understand the agent's context gathering steps; after following the agent's execution live, they only reviewed this context gathering process for 7\% of the agent's responses.
