\subsubsection{Overconfidence}
Another aspect of the agent's response that caused participants to mistrust it 
was the agent's conviction that the changes it made in its latest response correctly solved the issue. 
As P9 noted in the post-study questionnaire, 
\textit{``It never stopped thinking it knew how to solve the problem even though it was wrong"}. 
Even when the agent generated correct changes and explanations, 
its confidence in the alignments of these outputs to the issue sometimes 
led the participant to be hesitant of the agent. 
P18 noted about a generated fix 
that \textit{“it works, but its not really what I want”}. 
Similarly, upon reviewing the agent's description of files relevant to addressing the issue, P10 mentioned, 
\textit{``These things are true, but not necessarily things I want to modify}. 
Khati and colleagues~\cite{khati2025mappingtrustterrainllms} similarly report that AI trust metrics for developers include not only correctness, but also alignment with their intentions.

