\subsection{Collaborating Actively across the Development Process}
A recent blog post by Anthropic~\cite{anthropic2025} discusses the prevalence 
of the `feedback loop' interaction pattern, in which users collaborate 
with agents to work on code tasks simply by providing the agent with 
feedback from the environment. 
%TODONE Feels like "human router" 2.0; see https://knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu/article/can-deep-work-really-work/
In our study, we observed a similar interaction pattern, as many of participants' 
prompts requesting code changes consisted only of environmental context or 
details of the original issue description. 
However, our results suggest that such interactions are less effective 
than those in which developers provide their own expert knowledge to the agent (Section \ref{successcollab}). 
Thus, when collaborating with agents, developers should not restrict 
their role to `human routers' \cite{knowledgeatwharton2016} that simply provide environmental feedback, but rather actively contribute to the problem-solving process to achieve better results. Since participants often took a managerial role in these collaborations (Section \ref{sec:expert}), future work could explore how agents can adopt the communication strategies and behaviors exhibited by high-performing subordinate engineers.
% \gustavo{I think an example would be useful here.}

Further, our results also suggest that developers should not restrict their active involvement to the parts of the software engineering process in which the agent is not particularly helpful, such as debugging and testing.
% \gustavo{this is also a litle bit confusing. What `does not provide support` mean? It is unclear to me whether the tasks not performed by the agent were because of the lack of support or just because the agent failed to perform it, due to complexity or bad planning.} 
Even though participants primarily used the agent to generate code changes, 
they were more successful when they collaborated with the agent by also manually writing code. On the other hand, while participants expressed misgivings about the 
agent's ability to help with debugging (Section \ref{manact}), manually debugging and 
testing code did not appear to improve their likelihood of success. 

%In fact, participants who only worked on manually debugging rather 
% than coding performed worse than participants who worked only on 
% manual coding (2 out of 10 vs 3 out of 3 successful issues)\gustavo{I don't like this comparison because tasks that did not required debugging might have been just easier tasks. It is not very intuitive that debugging would harm success}.

% Thus, the agent's interactivity empowers participants to effectively intervene manually. 
Thus, developer-agent collaborations were most successful when both the developer and the agent actively contributed to issue resolution.
In line with prior findings~\cite{steeringmultiagent}, as the agent was not as supportive in debugging and localizing, it was often unable to actively contribute to this part of the issue resolution process, and thus
participants struggled to successfully resolve bugs.
While software engineering benchmarks have clear, 
well set up tasks and environments for agents to work on, 
real development can involve messy environmental setup and 
debugging steps before the developer is ready to make code changes. 
However, agents often fail at these tasks, as reflected in their 
worse performance on more realistic benchmarks~\cite{miserendino2025swelancerfrontierllmsearn}.
For SWE agents to be useful for developers in their day-to-day tasks, 
they must thus empower users across all the steps of the software engineering process, 
especially the messy and ambiguous ones.
