When $l=2$, more complex circuits can be generated where we choose the entanglement gates, $E_{\text{ent}}^{1}$ and $E_{\text{ent}}^{2}$, to be equivalent and the encoding layer takes the following form, $U_{\Phi(x)} =  E_{\text{ent}} U_{\phi_{2}(\mathbf{x})} E_{\text{ent}} U_{\phi_{1}(\mathbf{x})}$.
Additionally, we exclude IQP encoding when $l=2$ due to the increased computational costs associated with the circuit depth when compared to the expanded models based on A1, A2, and M encoding.
Therefore, when we exclude IQP, there are 5 unique combinations of $U_{\phi_{1}(\mathbf{x})}$ and $U_{\phi_{2}(\mathbf{x})}$ (M-M, A1-A1, A2-A2, M-A1, and M-A2) and two different entanglement layer options (CNOT and CZ) for a total of 10 encoding circuits with $l=2$.
