The next layer in a PQC, that follows the encoding layer, is the variational layer.
These layers introduce trainable parameters into the quantum circuit, enabling optimization through classical computation and providing flexibility to the QML models.
These layers have a general form, defined as,
\begin{equation}
	U(\bm{\theta}) = \prod_{v} U_{v}(\bm{\theta}_{v}), % E_{\text{ent}}^{v}
	\label{eq:general_variational}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\theta}$ denote the variational parameters, $v$ denote the number of times that the layer is repeated within the circuit, and all entanglement gates are implicitly included in $U_{v}(\bm{\theta}_{v})$.
In our study, we examine 12 variational circuits, found in \citet{sim_expressibility_2019}, as displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:ansatz}.
