\begin{abstract}
  Understanding and reasoning over academic handwritten notes remains a challenge in document AI, particularly for mathematical equations, diagrams, and scientific notations. Existing visual question answering (VQA) benchmarks focus on printed or structured handwritten text, limiting generalization to real-world note-taking. To address this, we introduce \textbf{\textsc{NoTeS-Bank}}, an \textit{evaluation benchmark} for \textbf{Neural Transcription and Search} in note-based question answering. \textsc{NoTeS-Bank} comprises complex notes across multiple domains, requiring models to process unstructured and multimodal content. The benchmark defines two tasks: (1) \textbf{Evidence-Based VQA}, where models retrieve localized answers with bounding-box evidence, and (2) \textbf{Open-Domain VQA}, where models classify the domain before retrieving relevant documents and answers. Unlike classical Document VQA datasets relying on optical character recognition (OCR) and structured data, \textsc{NoTeS-BANK} demands vision-language fusion, retrieval, and multimodal reasoning. We benchmark state-of-the-art Vision-Language Models (VLMs) and retrieval frameworks, exposing structured transcription and reasoning limitations. \textsc{NoTeS-Bank} provides a rigorous evaluation with \textit{NDCG@5, MRR, Recall@K, IoU, and ANLS}, establishing a new standard for visual document understanding and reasoning. 
  % Understanding academic handwritten notes, particularly mathematical equations, diagrams, and scientific notations, remains a significant challenge in Document AI. Existing Visual Question Answering (VQA) benchmarks predominantly address structured or printed texts, limiting their applicability to real-world, unstructured notes. To bridge this gap, we introduce \textbf{\textsc{NoTeS-Bank}}, a specialized evaluation benchmark for Neural Transcription and Search in note-based VQA tasks. \textsc{NoTeS-Bank} consists of complex, multimodal, and unstructured handwritten notes across diverse academic domains, requiring models to demonstrate advanced vision-language fusion and retrieval capabilities. The benchmark includes two core tasks: (1) Evidence-Based VQA, demanding localized answers supported by bounding-box evidence, and (2) Open-Domain VQA, involving domain classification and answer retrieval. Benchmarking state-of-the-art Vision-Language Models (VLMs), \textsc{NoTeS-Bank} highlights limitations in structured transcription and multimodal reasoning using comprehensive evaluation metrics.
  % The dataset and evaluation scripts will be publicly released upon acceptance.
\end{abstract}

